Hi Ben,

Just to clarify the scope of my comments: this applies to users who are 
using an installed copy of your package to create a report and have a 
subscription to Claude Code, not to anyone working inside your package 
source directory.

As far as I'm aware, those 3 files that you want to distribute with the 
Bioconductor package are typically expected to be located in the R 
project root for Claude Code to find them.  If, say, those files were 
put somewhere into inst/ and installed to the final package location on 
a non-developer user installation of your package, I'd expect that 
wouldn't have the same effect; I don't have a subscription to Claude 
Code to verify that.

Either way, what you may consider doing is write a function that 
symlinks those files into the user's current R project directory without 
overwriting existing files, similar to how the usethis package 
manipulates local files, and demonstrate it in your vignette as 
non-evaluated code.  Then it doesn't really matter where your files are 
located in your package directory (though I would suggest you consider 
maybe inst/scripts/ per 
contributions.bioconductor.org/docs.html?#doc-inst-scripts).  As a 
package reviewer, I would find that acceptable, although I don't have 
control over what packages are assigned to me and cannot speak for any 
of the other package reviewers.

